---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="About Spatial Displacement Theory - philosophy, development history, and how to contribute">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
    <!-- Header -->
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-display font-bold text-slate-900 mb-8">
      About SDT
    </h1>

    <div class="prose prose-slate prose-lg max-w-none">
      <h2>What SDT Is</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A geometric theory of matter</strong> &mdash; Particles are topological structures in a medium</li>
        <li><strong>A deterministic theory</strong> &mdash; All "quantum randomness" is measurement limitation</li>
        <li><strong>A unified theory</strong> &mdash; Same pressure equation describes atoms, planets, galaxies</li>
        <li><strong>An occlusion-based theory</strong> &mdash; Forces arise from mutual shadowing of background pressure</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What SDT Is NOT</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>NOT an interpretation of quantum mechanics</strong> &mdash; It's a replacement. No wavefunctions.</li>
        <li><strong>NOT a modification of general relativity</strong> &mdash; Spacetime doesn't curve.</li>
        <li><strong>NOT a hidden variable theory</strong> &mdash; Variables are directly observable in principle.</li>
        <li><strong>NOT pilot-wave theory</strong> &mdash; No guiding waves. Particles are extended vortices.</li>
        <li><strong>NOT an ether theory</strong> &mdash; Spation is relativistically consistent and undetectable except through displacement.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Development History</h2>
      <p>
        SDT has been developed over two years through intensive dialogue with multiple AI systems
        (Claude, GPT-4, others), serving as thought partners, calculation verifiers, and devil's advocates.
      </p>
      <p>
        The theory began as an attempt to derive atomic spectra from first principles without quantum
        postulates. When the same geometric framework unexpectedly produced correct planetary orbital
        velocities, the scope expanded to cover gravitation and cosmology.
      </p>

      <h2>Current Status</h2>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Benchmarks</strong></td>
            <td>15 of 24 certified</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Under Investigation</strong></td>
            <td>5 active</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Awaiting Development</strong></td>
            <td>4</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Peer Review</strong></td>
            <td>Not yet submitted</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Publication Target</strong></td>
            <td>PLOS ONE / Scientific Reports (2025)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Acknowledgments</h2>
      <p>Standing on shoulders:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Dirac</strong> &mdash; relativistic quantum mechanics</li>
        <li><strong>Bohm</strong> &mdash; hidden variables, deterministic QM</li>
        <li><strong>Wheeler</strong> &mdash; geometrodynamics</li>
        <li><strong>Milgrom</strong> &mdash; MOND, empirical galactic rotation</li>
        <li><strong>Verlinde</strong> &mdash; entropic gravity</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        While SDT differs fundamentally from all these approaches, they inspired the search
        for geometric foundations.
      </p>

      <h2>How to Contribute</h2>
      <p>We need:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Computational physicists:</strong> Optimize ray-tracing, implement fast multipole method</li>
        <li><strong>Astronomers:</strong> Test galactic predictions on SPARC database</li>
        <li><strong>Quantum physicists:</strong> Compare SDT vs QED for high-Z atoms</li>
        <li><strong>Mathematicians:</strong> Existence/uniqueness proofs for master equation</li>
        <li><strong>Code contributors:</strong> Python/Julia solvers, visualization tools</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/username/SDT">Contribute on GitHub &rarr;</a>
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Email:</strong> <a href="mailto:spatialdisplacementtheory@gmail.com">spatialdisplacementtheory@gmail.com</a></li>
        <li><strong>GitHub Issues:</strong> For technical questions and bug reports</li>
        <li><strong>Discussions:</strong> For general questions and ideas</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Citation</h2>
      <p>If using SDT in your work, please cite:</p>
      <div class="bg-slate-100 p-4 rounded-lg font-mono text-sm">
        Harvey, J.C. (2025). Spatial Displacement Theory: A Complete Derivation of Physics from Geometric Pressure Dynamics. Self-published. https://github.com/[repo] or DOI: [Zenodo DOI]
      </div>

      <h2>License</h2>
      <p>
        MIT License &mdash; Use freely, cite appropriately. We believe fundamental physics
        should be accessible to everyone.
      </p>
    </div>

